Technological determinism is an expression that includes a wide range of ideas in practice from technology as a driving force or technological necessity, to a narrow idea that the fate of man is driven by an obstructive logic related to scientific laws, as well as the way it translates into technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general ideas: that the development of technology in itself follows a route largely beyond cultural or political influence, and that technology in turn has an "influence" on societies that are hereditary and not socially conditioned.
For example, it can be said that the motor-powered car necessarily leads to the development of roads.
But a nation-wide road network isn't economically virtible for just a handful of cars, so new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of owning a car.
When many own cars, more accidents also happen on the roads, which leads to inventions of new techniques in the field of health to heal bodily injuries.
