Out on the savannah, it's hard for a primate with a digestive system such as humans to satisfy his need for amino acids from available plant sources.
In addition, there are serious repercussions of non-overhasis: decreased growth, malnutrition and, ultimately, death.
The most easily available plant resources would have been the proteins that exist in leaves and legumes, but for primates like us, these are hard to digest unless prepared.
Conversely, animal feed (Anies, termites, eggs) isn't just easy-to-pest. It also provides large amounts of protein, which contains all the essential amino acids.
All in all, it shouldn't surprise us if our own ancestors solved their "protein problem" in roughly the same way as chimpanzees on the savannah do it today.
Sleep Disruption is the process of deliberately waking up during your normal period of sleep and falling asleep a short time later (10-60 minutes).
